# Reachability allow-list — symbols the application defines that are
# deliberately (or, for now, knowingly) absent from the linked image.
#
# Read scripts/reachability.sh first for the mechanism. This file is the
# policy, and the policy is a BASELINE FREEZE, not a blessing:
#
#   * every symbol here is absent from zephyr.elf today;
#   * a NEW absence — a function added with no caller — is not here, so the
#     build fails and someone has to decide;
#   * an entry that stops being absent is also an error, so this file cannot
#     rot into a rubber stamp.
#
# WHY A FREEZE RATHER THAN A CLEAN SHEET. This project shipped, in one
# session, a firmware-update orchestrator, a skyplot renderer, PTP Annex-P
# integrity, the whole Rb serial layer and about fourteen operator recovery
# actions — all written, all tested, all documented as delivered, and all
# discarded by --gc-sections because nothing called them. Host tests cannot
# see it: they link core/ directly and never touch the target image. Wiring
# them up took the absent count from 320 to 218. The remaining 218 are not
# all fine, and pretending otherwise by inventing a category for each would
# reproduce the exact failure this file exists to stop. So the ones with a
# defensible reason carry it, and the rest say TODO and are honest backlog.
#
# AUDIT STATUS — WHICH REASONS HAVE BEEN CHECKED AND WHICH HAVE NOT.
# The first version of this file assigned reasons in alphabetical blocks, and
# several of them were false. An adversarial audit went through it. What that
# audit reached, and what it did not:
#
#   AUDITED per symbol, against the linked image and the source:
#     inlined (12)     each one located in zephyr.elf — method below
#     no-caller (42)   reference set established for each; see that block
#     ATECC (18)       regrouped; the old blanket reason explained five of them
#     superseded (3)   the _profile twins confirmed present in the image
#     oracle (12)      11 of 11 held, plus the already-checked mp_cbor_rd_text.
#                      See that block for what the audit established.
#     fmt (15)         audited one at a time, and the blanket reason is GONE:
#                      it was wrong for seven of the eighteen. One was not
#                      absent-by-design at all but a real operability gap and
#                      is now BOUND (ptp_profile_deviation_text, removed from
#                      this file); one was not a formatter and is now in
#                      `inlined` (fwupd_comp_desc); one is an accessor and is
#                      now TODO (fwupd_end_reason); five kept the conclusion
#                      but lost a false premise. See that block.
#
#   NOT INDEPENDENTLY AUDITED — the reason on each line is asserted, not
#   checked, and should be read as an untested claim:
#     TODO (110)       declared backlog. Nothing to audit — that is the point.
#
#   The `ntske_* (6)` line that used to sit in this block is gone, and how it
#   went is worth keeping: it said the *deferral* was verified but that "these
#   six are the complete set was not re-derived". Enabling NTS-KE settled both
#   halves at once. Five of the six went into the image and this gate caught
#   them as stale; the sixth was not deferred at all, it was inlined, and it is
#   now in the inlined block with the disassembly that proves it. The
#   unre-derived claim was the accurate part: the set was six because the
#   feature was off, not because anyone had checked what six meant.
#
# THE CATEGORIES:
#
#   fmt         enum->string formatters with no device-side caller. AUDITED,
#               and the single blanket reason that used to stand here — "device
#               logs the code, host renders the name" — is deliberately not
#               replaced by another one, because it was false for seven of the
#               eighteen entries and no single sentence is true of the rest.
#               Every line now carries its own reason. The standing
#               qualification on the ones that DO say "host renders the name":
#               that half rests on the out-of-tree FMT GUI (docs/
#               sts1000_field_maintenance_tool.md §10). tools/meridian_ctl.py,
#               the in-tree host tool, carries name tables for CMD/ERR/CAP/
#               SLOT/GROUPS/TYPE/CFG_FLAG/LEVELS/SUBSYS and for NONE of these
#               enums — so for anything meridian_ctl.py can reach, the name is
#               rendered nowhere at all today.
#   oracle      the CBOR *reader*. This device is a CBOR writer — telemetry
#               goes device->host, the control plane is JSON-RPC. The reader
#               is how test_mp_stream.c and test_mp_mirror.c decode what the
#               writer produced and prove the round trip. AUDITED, 11 of 11 (the
#               twelfth, mp_cbor_rd_text, had already been checked): `grep -rln
#               "mp_cbor_rd" app/src/` returns only mp_stream.h and mp_stream.c,
#               so nothing on the device reads CBOR; the mirror builds with the
#               writer API and mp_mirror_encode is in the image at 0x080217f0.
#               Eight are named by no relocation anywhere in libapp.a. The three
#               that ARE named — mp_cbor_skip from mp_cbor_map_find at
#               mp_stream.c:596, and the rest of that group — form a closed dead
#               subtree: mp_cbor_map_find is itself absent from zephyr.elf, so
#               its callees die with it.
#   inlined     the instructions SHIP; only the separately-addressable global
#               entry point was collected. This is a strictly stronger claim
#               than any other category here — the others say "nothing calls
#               it", this one says "the code is in the image, at this address".
#               It is earned one of two ways and every line says which:
#                 (a) a .part/.isra/.constprop clone located at an address in
#                     zephyr.elf and called from a function that is itself in
#                     the image (GCC partial inlining); or
#                 (b) full inlining — a call site exists in the source, NO
#                     relocation anywhere in libapp.a names the symbol, and the
#                     enclosing caller is in the image; confirmed by
#                     disassembling that caller where the callee's work is
#                     small enough to recognise.
#               The caller-in-image check in (b) is the load-bearing one: it is
#               what separates "inlined into live code" from "referenced only
#               by other dead code", and dropping it is what would let a whole
#               dead subtree pose as shipped.
#   no-caller   the reference set has been established and it contains no
#               production caller: no relocation anywhere in libapp.a names the
#               symbol and no call site exists under app/ — or the only call
#               sites are inside functions that are themselves absent. The
#               named host suite is the sole exerciser. This is a FINDING, not
#               an absolution. It blesses nothing that TODO does not; several
#               of these are things an operator surface arguably ought to be
#               calling. What it buys over TODO is that the next reader does
#               not have to repeat the search.
#   deferred    a feature deliberately not enabled in this image, with the
#               reason recorded at the point of deferral.
#   superseded  a base variant whose _profile twin is what the engine calls.
#               Verified present in the image.
#
#   TODO        no decision yet. A standing action. Most of these are status
#               accessors that an operator surface arguably should be
#               reporting and does not — which is a gap, not a category.
#
# To regenerate the absent set after a build:
#   scripts/reachability.sh --update
# and then justify, or wire up, whatever it marks TODO.

# --- formatters -----------------------------------------------------
# AUDITED per symbol. The old blanket reason is gone; see AUDIT STATUS above for
# why it is not replaced with another one.
#
# ptp_profile_deviation_text is no longer here. It was not a host-side formatter
# and it was not deliberately absent: ptp.h, ptp_profile.h and ptp_profile.c all
# promised it "reports the deviation to the operator" while it was called from
# nothing but three lines of test_ptp_profile.c. It now has production callers
# (sts_ptp.c's start-up log line, sts_web.c's pv_ptp(), sts_snmp.c) and is in the
# image, so an entry here would be stale and this gate would fail on it.
#
# The five below keep their conclusion — no device-side caller — but the premise
# "device logs the code, host renders the name" was false for every one of them:
# the device never logs or transmits the code, so no host can render anything.
atecc_status_name                      # fmt: no-caller. core/atecc does no logging at all; atecc.c:567/949 pass the byte to atecc_status_to_errno() and discard it, so the code never leaves the device
ina228_cause_str                       # fmt: no-caller. INA228_CAUSE_* appears nowhere outside core/ina228, and its producer ina228_diag_cause() is itself allow-listed no-caller
ldap_result_name                       # fmt: no-caller BY DESIGN, and the only entry here whose absence is a security property. sts_aaa.c:1182 does ldap_result_to_errno(r.code) and never logs the code because FMT spec sts1000_field_maintenance_tool.md §5.3 makes a wrong password, an unknown user, an over-long field and "no authority could answer" one indistinguishable refusal (anti-enumeration). Naming the LDAP result would undo that. Do not "fix" this by adding a log line
pwrseq_alarm_name                      # fmt: no-caller, but the old reason ("pwrseq_alarms(), pwrseq_status() and pwrseq_stage() are all absent, so nothing reads the alarm word this would name") is now FALSE: pwrseq_status() is bound (pwrseq_exec.c pwrseq_publish) and the word goes out on MP telemetry key 46 and reaches sts_pwrseq_snap_t::alarms. It is still absent because the device transmits the bitmap and the host renders the names — tools/meridian_ctl.py carries no table for pwrseq_alarm_t, so today the name is rendered only by the out-of-tree FMT GUI
ubx_fwupd_state_name                   # fmt: no-caller. Its producer ubx_fwupd_state() is allow-listed TODO and absent from the image
# The rest keep the original reason: an MP-plane or UI formatter the device does
# not call, whose name is rendered host-side. Qualification above applies.
mp_diag_ev_name                        # fmt: enum->string; device logs the code, host renders the name
mp_ev_kind_name                        # fmt: enum->string; device logs the code, host renders the name
mp_ilk_reason                          # fmt: enum->string; device logs the code, host renders the name
ptp_msg_type_name                      # fmt: enum->string; device logs the code, host renders the name
ptp_port_state_name                    # fmt: enum->string; device logs the code, host renders the name
ptp_recommended_name                   # fmt: enum->string; device logs the code, host renders the name
pwrseq_action_name                     # fmt: enum->string; device logs the code, host renders the name
refsel_action_name                     # fmt: enum->string; device logs the code, host renders the name
ui_sky_view_name                       # fmt: enum->string; device logs the code, host renders the name
ui_surface_row_text                    # fmt: enum->string; device logs the code, host renders the name

# --- inlined: the code ships, only the global entry point was collected ---
# Addresses are from the audited build. They will move; the shape will not.
rb_serial_mode                         # inlined(b): sts_rb_serial_status() @0x08041d90 calls it at rb_serial.c:509; no reloc in libapp.a names it
sky_colour_for_sys                     # inlined(b): sky_render() @0x08096136 calls it at skyplot.c:784; no reloc in libapp.a names it
wss_sha1                               # inlined(a): wss_sha1.part.0 @0x0803b558, called from wss_accept_key @0x0803b63c (bl at 0x0803b684); the 8-byte global is a guard+tail-jump thunk
ubx_checksum                           # inlined(a): ubx_checksum.part.0 @0x08094f3e, called from ubx_frame @0x08094f68 and ubx_valset_end @0x080957e8 — the checksum runs on every frame
http_span_eq                           # inlined(b): one-line forwarder onto web_span_eq() @0x08098282; http_parse_request @0x08035be8 relocates against the forwardee, not this
http_span_eq_ci                        # inlined(b): same, onto web_span_eq_ci() @0x0809823e; ~20 call sites in http_parse_request, every one resolved to the forwardee
ntp_short_from_q16                     # inlined(b): saturating cast at ntp.c:1325-1326 inside ntp_handle_request() @0x080303c4
snmp_rd_done                           # inlined(b): snmp.c:1614 inside snmp__parse_pdu() @0x08093cbc
snmp_wr_rewind                         # inlined(b): snmp.c:1383/1401/1433/1461 inside snmp__emit_response() @0x08031a58
ina228_device_id_die                   # inlined(b): ina228.c:739 inside ina228_id_matches() @0x0808c35c, which disassembles to `lsrs r0, r1, #4` — the >>4 extract, no call
# fwupd_comp_desc was filed under `fmt` as an absent formatter. Both halves of
# that were false: fwupd.h:217 declares it returning `const fwupd_comp_desc_t *`,
# not a name, and it is not absent — it SHIPS, inlined into fwupd_comp_name.
fwupd_comp_desc                        # inlined(b): fwupd_comp_name() @0x080271a8 carries the whole body — `cmp r0,#10` (bound vs FWUPD_COMP__COUNT), `movls r2,#20` (descriptor stride), `mlals r3,r2,r0,r3` (&comp_tbl[comp]), `ldrls r0,[r3,#4]` (d->name). Every reloc naming it in libapp.a is in .debug_{info,line,frame,aranges,loc,ranges}; no .text section relocates against the symbol
# ntske_exporter_context was in the "deferred" block until NTS-KE was enabled.
# It is not deferred and it is not uncalled: it is the only one of the six that
# the compiler swallowed whole. Every reference to it in libapp.a is in
# .debug_{info,line,frame,aranges,loc,ranges} — no .text section relocates
# against it — and its two call sites (nts.c:904/911, in static ntske_derive(),
# itself inlined) are in ntske_handle @0x08030fc8, which IS in the image.
# Its three stores are visible there: `mov.w sl,#0x0f000000` + `str.w sl,[sp,#108]`
# is bytes_put_be16(out[0], proto NTPV4=0) and bytes_put_be16(out[2], aead 15
# = AES-SIV-CMAC-256); `strb.w r3,[sp,#112]` with r3=0 then r3=1 is
# `out[4] = c2s ? 0x00 : 0x01`. The 5-byte buffer is then passed as (r2, r3=5)
# to the exporter with r1 = 0x080c0262 = "EXPORTER-network-time-security".
ntske_exporter_context                 # inlined(b): nts.c:904/911 via static ntske_derive() inside ntske_handle() @0x08030fc8; only .debug_* relocs name it

# --- no production caller -------------------------------------------
#
# core/stats first, because it is the one block here that is absent by *design
# decision* rather than by drift, and the decision should be legible without
# reading the module.
#
# core/stats is the spec §14 stability-analysis core: overlapping ADEV, MDEV, and
# the PPS residual histogram, over a phase or residual record exported from the
# device and analysed on a host. It is deliberately not bound into the image, and
# the alternative was considered rather than assumed:
#
#   * The runtime already has the stability telemetry it needs, and it is not
#     this. core/disc's disc_adev_overlapping() runs on the 1 Hz discipline
#     thread in float over a bounded ring and publishes adev_1s/10s/100s into the
#     quality block. That is present in the image and reached by adev_update().
#     core/stats is the offline counterpart, not a replacement — different
#     precision, different tau set, different caller.
#
#   * Binding core/stats would put double-precision arithmetic on this part. The
#     STM32H563's FPU is FPv5-*SP*: every double operation is a soft-float call.
#     A multi-decade ADEV+MDEV sweep over a long record in soft double, to
#     produce numbers no on-board consumer reads, is the wrong trade — and the
#     double is not negotiable, because the estimator's dynamic range is what
#     makes it correct (see the arithmetic rationale in stats/adev.h).
#
#   * So the reachable-by-construction options were both worse than absence: a
#     fake caller, or a smaller/wronger estimator. The honest entry is this one.
#
# If a future §14 deliverable does want on-device ADEV/MDEV vs tau — a TFT
# stability page, or an MCP telemetry key — the fix is a real caller and these
# seven lines come out, not a wider reason here.
stats_adev                             # no-caller: §14 bench analysis, host-side. test_stats.c only
stats_mdev                             # no-caller: §14 bench analysis, host-side. test_stats.c only. The runtime has no MDEV at all and needs none — MDEV separates white PM from flicker PM, which is a diagnosis made at the bench, not a servo input
stats_dev_sweep                        # no-caller: the tau-axis wrapper over stats_adev/stats_mdev; nothing on-target plots a sweep
stats_m_octaves                        # no-caller: generates the octave tau axis for stats_dev_sweep(), itself absent
stats_max_m                            # no-caller: reached only from stats_m_octaves(), itself absent
stats_hist                             # no-caller: §14 PPS residual histogram. The device streams pulse records over MP and the host bins them; binning on-device would discard the residual series that is the actual evidence
stats_sqrt                             # no-caller: core/ has no libm (see quality.h), so the module carries its own double square root. Public rather than static so test_stats.c can assert its accuracy against an independent Newton reference directly instead of inferring it. Its only callers are stats_adev/stats_mdev/stats_hist, all absent
#
# core/stats' phase-record codec (stats/phase_rec.h) splits across the boundary,
# and the split is the whole point of the module: the DEVICE encodes and the HOST
# decodes. phase_rec_encode_f / phase_rec_size / phase_rec_size_flags /
# phase_rec_ns_f_to_ps are therefore NOT in this file — they have a real caller in
# the image (disc_publish_phase() in the discipline thread, serving MCP
# PHASE_EXPORT 0x24). The rest are the reader half, plus the two encoder entry
# points the device does not use. Binding the readers would mean inventing an
# on-device consumer of a record the device itself produced, which is a fake
# caller by construction.
#
# Their real caller is tools/meridian_phase.c, built by tests/host/CMakeLists.txt
# and exercised by the `phase_cli` CTest entry — the same C the device encodes
# with, which is the reason the format has one implementation rather than two.
#
# NOTE: phase_rec_encode was in the image until the record gained its second
# (uncorrected) series. The exporter now calls phase_rec_encode_f(), which takes
# the discipline loop's float rings directly and converts inside the encoder —
# that is what let the paired record cost 4 KiB of RAM instead of 8, by deleting
# the int64 staging array the old call needed. phase_rec_encode is the
# single-series entry point and is now host-only.
phase_rec_encode                       # no-caller: single-series encoder. The device writes PAIRED records (spec §14 needs the with/without histograms from one export), so disc_publish_phase() calls phase_rec_encode_f() instead. Kept because the host tool and the suites build single-series records to prove the decoder refuses one that CLAIMS the pair
phase_rec_encode_pair                  # no-caller: the int64 pair encoder. The device holds its rings as float nanoseconds and goes through phase_rec_encode_f(); this is the form the host tool's self-test and test_saw.c build synthetic paired records with
phase_rec_decode_hdr                   # no-caller: host-side reader. Validates magic/version/length and cross-checks the gap count against the bitmap; the device wrote those fields and has nothing to validate them for
phase_rec_decode_samples               # no-caller: host-side reader, pulls the int64 ps series out for stats_adev/stats_mdev — both themselves absent for the reasons above
phase_rec_decode_raw                   # no-caller: host-side reader for the UNCORRECTED series (PHASE_REC_F_RAW). The device wrote it; the half of §14's sawtooth proof that reads it back runs on the bench
phase_rec_gap_at                       # no-caller: host-side reader, per-sample gap query behind the CLI's gap listing
phase_rec_longest_clean                # no-caller: host-side reader. Finds the longest uniformly spaced run in a record the runtime absorbed gaps into, so a bench operator can salvage a usable window instead of discarding the capture
#
# stats/saw.h is spec §14's sawtooth verdict: given the corrected and uncorrected
# residual series out of one phase record, decide whether the qErr path collapsed
# the spread (working), roughly doubled it (sign inverted) or did nothing. It sits
# in core/ rather than in the tool for the reason the estimators do — one proved
# implementation, not a second one in the analyser that is free to disagree.
#
# It is host-side for the same reason stats_hist is: the device HAS both series,
# but judging them on-target would mean shipping the verdict instead of the
# evidence, and the evidence is what a bench operator needs when the verdict is
# SIGN-INVERTED. Its caller is tools/meridian_phase.c (`phase_cli`), with
# tests/host/test_saw.c pinning the three signatures against their closed forms.
stats_saw_verdict                      # no-caller: §14 with/without sawtooth comparison, host-side. Nothing on-target consumes a verdict about its own qErr path — it would have no action to take on one, and the record the bench reads is strictly more informative
stats_saw_str                          # no-caller: names the verdict for CLI output; reached only from the analyser, alongside stats_saw_verdict itself
#
# INA228 next. The old reason claimed "the device writes, the host interprets";
# the opposite is true. hk_read_ina() converts on-board (hk.c:590-615) using
# ina228_raw16/raw20_unsigned/raw24 with ina228_current_lsb_pa() and
# ina228_power_lsb_nw(). These entries are the *alternative* one-shot unit
# conversions and the register-encode inverses, which that path never calls.
ina228_current_ua                      # no-caller: test_ina228.c only
ina228_current_na                      # no-caller: reached only from ina228_current_ua() (ina228.c:521), itself absent
ina228_raw20_signed                    # no-caller: reached only from ina228_current_na()/ina228_vshunt_nv(), both absent
ina228_raw40                           # no-caller: reached only from ina228_energy_uj()/ina228_raw40_signed(), both absent
ina228_raw40_signed                    # no-caller: reached only from ina228_charge_nc() (ina228.c:559), itself absent
ina228_vbus_uv                         # no-caller: reached only from ina228_vbus_mv() (ina228.c:493), itself absent
ina228_vbus_mv                         # no-caller: hk.c:415 does the 195.3125 uV scaling inline instead
ina228_vshunt_nv                       # no-caller: test_ina228.c only
ina228_power_nw                        # no-caller: hk.c uses ina228_power_lsb_nw() + ina228_raw24() instead
ina228_energy_uj                       # no-caller: test_ina228.c only
ina228_charge_nc                       # no-caller: test_ina228.c only
ina228_dietemp_mc                      # no-caller: test_ina228.c only
ina228_config_decode                   # no-caller: the encode direction ships (ina228_config_encode); this inverse is the round-trip oracle
ina228_adc_config_decode               # no-caller: same, inverse of ina228_adc_config_encode()
ina228_diag_encode                     # no-caller: the decode direction ships (ina228_diag_decode); this inverse is the round-trip oracle
ina228_diag_cause                      # no-caller: hk.c reads flags via ina228_diag_decode() at hk.c:640
ina228_diag_is_fault                   # no-caller: hk.c uses ina228_diag_fault_mask() at hk.c:667
ina228_bus_code                        # no-caller: test_ina228.c only
ina228_shunt_code                      # no-caller: reached only from ina228_shunt_code_ma()/ina228_rail_sovl_default(), both absent
ina228_shunt_code_ma                   # no-caller: test_ina228.c only
ina228_shunt_code_to_ua                # no-caller: test_ina228.c only
ina228_temp_code                       # no-caller: test_ina228.c only
ina228_power_code                      # no-caller: test_ina228.c only
ina228_reg_width                       # no-caller: hk.c passes the width per register at each ina_read()
ina228_avg_samples                     # no-caller: reached only from ina228_conv_interval_us() (ina228.c:244), itself absent
ina228_ct_us                           # no-caller: reached only from ina228_conv_interval_us() (ina228.c:235/238/241), itself absent
ina228_conv_interval_us                # no-caller: test_ina228.c only
ina228_fs_current_ua                   # no-caller: test_ina228.c only
ina228_device_id_rev                   # no-caller: ina228_id_matches() gates on the die code, not the revision
ina228_rail_by_addr                    # no-caller: hk.c indexes ina228_rail_tbl[] by ina228_rail_t, never by I2C address
ina228_rail_sovl_default               # no-caller: test_ina228.c only; hk.c programs no SOVL threshold
# The rest: no relocation in libapp.a names it and no call site exists under
# app/. The named suite is the only exerciser.
aes_cmac                               # no-caller: public one-shot that only test_aes_siv.c calls — AES-SIV's s2v() drives cmac_begin/update/final directly (aes_siv.c:209,219,229); it is a CMAC primitive, not a decoder
cobs_encode_max                        # no-caller: test_cobs.c only; mp_frame.c:79 sizes with sizeof(tx->enc)
cobs_decode_max                        # no-caller: test_cobs.c only; same
http_path_decode                       # no-caller: test_http_parse.c only; http_pct_decode() @0x08097880 is the live entry point
ptp_ts_to_ns                           # no-caller: test_ptp_msg.c only; ptp_ts_from_ns() @0x08021f08 is the live direction — a grandmaster writes timestamps
ring_cap                               # no-caller: test_ring.c only; the live path uses ring_len()/ring_free()
ring_empty                             # no-caller: test_ring.c only; same
ring_full                              # no-caller: test_ring.c only; same
snmp_rd_uint                           # no-caller: test_snmp.c only; the live decoder uses snmp_rd_int() @0x0809347a
snmp_val_ip                            # no-caller: test_snmp.c only; no IpAddress-typed OID in the shipped MIB — sts_snmp.c builds every value with snmp_val_int()/snmp_val_str()
web_b64_decode                         # no-caller: test_rest.c only; the web plane only encodes (the WSS accept key), and the MP plane carries its own mp_b64_decode() @0x0802b0bc

# --- CBOR read direction --------------------------------------------
# NOT INDEPENDENTLY AUDITED except mp_cbor_rd_text — see AUDIT STATUS above.
mp_cbor_map_find                       # oracle: device is a CBOR writer; the reader is the tests' round-trip oracle
mp_cbor_peek_major                     # oracle: device is a CBOR writer; the reader is the tests' round-trip oracle
mp_cbor_rd_arr                         # oracle: device is a CBOR writer; the reader is the tests' round-trip oracle
mp_cbor_rd_bool                        # oracle: device is a CBOR writer; the reader is the tests' round-trip oracle
mp_cbor_rd_bytes                       # oracle: device is a CBOR writer; the reader is the tests' round-trip oracle
