# --- deliberately deferred features ---------------------------------
# NTS-KE is no longer one of them. Five of the six ntske_* entries that stood
# here — ntske_handle, ntske_init, ntske_rec_parse, ntske_rec_put and
# ntske_rec_put_u16 — are in the image now that conf/net.conf enables the
# exporter, and this gate is what proved it: it failed on all five as stale the
# first time the feature was switched on. The sixth moved up to the inlined
# block, where its evidence is. Nothing else changed category.
ldap_build_starttls                    # deferred: StartTLS needs an in-place socket upgrade Zephyr's TLS type cannot do

# --- ATECC608B ------------------------------------------------------
# The old blanket reason — "ATECC-backed TLS key is a permanently-open item
# (spec 15.2)" — was wrong twice over. The citation resolved to nothing:
# software_spec §15.2 lists three open items and none of them is this. The
# record is ARCHITECTURE.md §5. And the reason was stretched across eighteen
# symbols when it explains five. Each group below carries what is true for it.
#
# What the part IS the root for, and what is therefore NOT absent:
# sts_atecc_serial(), sts_atecc_device_unique(), sts_atecc_attest(),
# sts_atecc_counter_increment() and atecc_pubkey() are all in the image.
#
# The deferral proper: routing the HTTPS / NTS-KE server identity to the secure
# element needs an mbedTLS PK_OPAQUE / PSA driver over CryptoAuthLib. These are
# exactly the key-slot operations that driver would consume, and nothing else
# reaches them.
sts_atecc_sign                         # deferred: ATECC-backed TLS server key (ARCHITECTURE.md §5) — the PK_OPAQUE sign entry; zero callers anywhere
sts_atecc_pubkey                       # deferred: ATECC-backed TLS server key (ARCHITECTURE.md §5); zero callers anywhere
sts_atecc_ecdh                         # deferred: ATECC-backed TLS server key (ARCHITECTURE.md §5); zero callers anywhere
atecc_sign                             # deferred: same; reached only from sts_atecc_sign() at sts_atecc.c:350
atecc_ecdh                             # deferred: same; reached only from sts_atecc_ecdh() at sts_atecc.c:403
atecc_nonce_passthrough                # intra-TU only: sole callers are atecc_sign() (atecc.c:699) and atecc_verify_extern() (atecc.c:721), both themselves absent — it dies with them
# The rest are NOT explained by that deferral. Checked one at a time: the TRNG,
# status accessors, power management, crypto offload, provisioning.
atecc_random                           # TODO: NOT the device CSPRNG — sys_csrand_get() on the H5 RNG peripheral is (portz_crypto.c:137); reached only from sts_atecc_random(), itself uncalled
sts_atecc_random                       # TODO: zero callers anywhere; wire it up as an entropy source or delete it. ARCHITECTURE.md §5 used to claim the part was the TRNG root; it is not
atecc_present                          # TODO: status accessor; reached only from sts_atecc_present() at sts_atecc.c:258
sts_atecc_present                      # TODO: status accessor, zero callers; no operator surface reports whether the part is fitted
atecc_sleep                            # TODO: power management, zero callers; the glue idles instead — atecc_idle() at sts_atecc.c:217 and :464
sts_atecc_verify                       # TODO: external-signature verify offload, zero callers; verification runs in software
atecc_verify_extern                    # TODO: reached only from sts_atecc_verify() at sts_atecc.c:384, itself uncalled
atecc_sha256                           # TODO: hash offload, zero callers
atecc_hmac                             # TODO: HMAC offload, zero callers
atecc_lock                             # TODO: provisioning command, zero callers; the firmware never locks a zone
atecc_write_zone                       # TODO: provisioning command, zero callers
atecc_genkey_private                   # TODO: provisioning command, zero callers; the key is expected to be pre-provisioned

