# sts_mp_notify_link is no longer here: sts_usb.c calls it on every CDC-ACM #0
# DTR transition and on VBUS loss, which is what makes a closed console port
# revert the overrides and hand the shell back. Its two neighbours below stayed,
# and the reasons are structural rather than backlog — both were audited against
# the Zephyr 4.2 sources named in them, and both have a matching correction in
# docs/sts1000_field_maintenance_tool.md so the doc and the image agree.
sts_mp_notify_break                    # no-caller, UNCALLABLE on this board rather than unwired. `zephyr,shell-uart` is cdc_acm_uart0 and there is no physical console UART, so a BREAK would have to arrive one of two ways and neither exists: cdc_acm_driver_api (subsys/usb/device/class/cdc_acm.c) publishes no `.err_check`, so z_impl_uart_err_check() returns -ENOSYS and UART_BREAK is unreadable; and cdc_acm_class_handle_req() implements only SET_LINE_CODING and SET_CONTROL_LINE_STATE, so a host's USB CDC SEND_BREAK is answered -ENOTSUP with no callback and nothing recorded. Kept as the seam a physical console would use; the shell's mode-entry banner no longer offers BREAK as an escape
sts_mp_shell_tap                       # no-caller, and structurally unfeedable rather than pending. On ACM1 it would be wrong: MP transmits through uart_poll_out(DT_CHOSEN(zephyr_shell_uart)), so a magic accepted on the MCP channel answers on a port the sender is not reading. On ACM0 the Zephyr shell owns the bytes and its only seam, shell_set_bypass(), is all-or-nothing with no way to hand one back; the byte-level diversion in shell_uart.c's uart_rx_handle() is compiled in only under CONFIG_MCUMGR_TRANSPORT_SHELL and hardcoded to smp_shell_rx_bytes(). Full argument in the wiring block of src/zephyr/console/mp_glue.c
